1. **Create a `RootObjectMapper`** with the default settings you asked for  
   (dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, numeric detection).  
2. **Serialize it to XContent** using the helper you provided, passing an  
   `XContentBuilder` instance and a `java.util.ArrayList` that tells the method to  
   *include defaults* and *skip runtime fields*.

```java
// 1. Build the RootObjectMapper with the required defaults
RootObjectMapper rootObjectMapper = new RootObjectMapper(
        /* name */ "_doc",
        /* type */ null,
        /* mapping */ new MappingBuilder()
                .setDynamicDateFormats(Arrays.asList("strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"))
                .setDynamicTemplates(Arrays.asList(/* your dynamic templates here */))
                .setDateDetection(true)
                .setNumericDetection(true)
                .build()
);

// 2. Prepare the XContentBuilder instance (you already have one)
XContentBuilder xContentBuilderInstance = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();

// 3. Prepare the parameters ArrayList
ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // includeDefaults
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // skipRuntimeFields

// 4. Call the helper – note that the helper expects *string* representations
//    of the objects, so we convert them to strings.
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(new HashMap<String, Object>() {{
    put("builder", xContentBuilderInstance.toString());   // <-- string representation
    put("params", params.toString());                     // <-- string representation
}});
```

