| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| 1 | A `RootObjectMapper` is instantiated with the defaults you requested: <br>• `dynamic_date_formats` set to `"strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"` <br>• `dynamic_templates` populated with whatever you supply <br>• `date_detection` and `numeric_detection` turned on | These are the defaults you wanted to serialize. |
| 2 | An `XContentBuilder` instance (`xContentBuilderInstance`) is created. | This is the builder that will receive the serialized JSON. |
| 3 | An `ArrayList` called `params` is built with two `Boolean.TRUE` entries. | The first entry tells the serializer to *include defaults*; the second tells it to *skip runtime fields*. |
| 4 | The helper `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` is invoked. | The helper expects the builder and params as **string representations**, so we pass `toString()` of each. |

> **Tip:** If you’re working in a real Java environment, you can skip the `toString()` conversions and call the method directly on the `RootObjectMapper` instance:

```java
rootObjectMapper.doXContent(xContentBuilderInstance, params);
```

But because the tool you’re using requires string inputs, the snippet above follows that contract.

